INT. CONVERTED GRAIN SILO - ARBITRATION HALL - DAY

A round concrete chamber, forty feet across, with a ladder bolted to the wall and a hopper chute high above. A plank bench for the public. A folding table for the court. Dust floats in a single shaft of light.

JUDGE PHAEDRA LOMAX, 60s, in a rumpled black robe over a flannel shirt, sits behind the table. She keeps a thermos of tea at her elbow and a gavel carved from a fence post.

SIMONE DUCHAMP, 40s, lead counsel for the farmers, in a tailored green suit, rises, a stack of exhibits hugged to her chest.

TARIQ NASSAR, 40s, counsel for the agribusiness, in a charcoal suit and silk tie, taps his pen against his notepad.

CLETUS WREN, 60s, the bailiff, stands at the ladder with his thumbs hooked in his belt. He has not smiled since the Carter administration.

In a single armchair at the side sits the FOREMAN, 50s, a man in overalls and a seed-company cap, with a clipboard in his lap. He chews a toothpick.

LOMAX
We are on the record. Ms. Duchamp.

DUCHAMP
Your Honour, we ask the jury for a verdict on count one.

LOMAX
There is no jury, Ms. Duchamp. There is Mr. Foreman.

DUCHAMP
Then we ask the Foreman.

FOREMAN
Ma'am, I got a question first.

LOMAX
Proceed.

FOREMAN
Do I have to say it out loud?

LOMAX
That is how verdicts work.

FOREMAN
Even if it makes someone mad?

LOMAX
Especially then.

He removes the cap. Smooths his hair. Puts the cap back on.

NASSAR
Your Honour, I object. The witness is stalling.

LOMAX
He is not a witness, Mr. Nassar. Sit down.

NASSAR
I'd like it noted for the record that--

LOMAX
Noted. Sit.

He sits. Duchamp's grip on her exhibits tightens.

DUCHAMP
Mr. Foreman, take your time.

FOREMAN
Don't need it, ma'am.

He stands. Clears his throat. The toothpick rolls to the side of his mouth.

FOREMAN (CONT'D)
On count one, the soil contamination, we find for the farmers.

A murmur on the bench. Nassar's pen stops. Duchamp closes her eyes.

LOMAX
Order.

NASSAR
Your Honour, I demand the jury be polled.

LOMAX
There is one man, Mr. Nassar. He just spoke.

NASSAR
Then I demand he be re-sworn.

LOMAX
Mr. Wren, please re-swear Mr. Foreman.

WREN
Raise your right hand.

The Foreman raises his hand. Wren raises his, solemn.

WREN (CONT'D)
Do you swear to render a true verdict according to the evidence?

FOREMAN
I do.

WREN
State your name for the record.

FOREMAN
Hiram Stroud.

HIRAM
Hiram Stroud, Dutch Creek. Third generation on the same acres.

LOMAX
Thank you, Mr. Stroud. Please repeat your verdict.

HIRAM
On count one we find for the farmers.

NASSAR
He's biased. His own land is in the class.

HIRAM
It is. And it's poisoned, sir. I tested it myself.

Nassar half rises, shoulders squared. Hiram does not move. Wren steps forward. Duchamp slides between them. The shaft of light falls across all four faces.

LOMAX
Sit down, Mr. Nassar.

He does. He opens his notepad. Closes it. Opens it again.

LOMAX (CONT'D)
Ms. Duchamp. Count two.

DUCHAMP
The farmers move for punitive damages.

HIRAM
We find for the farmers on count two.

NASSAR
You haven't heard the evidence!

HIRAM
I've heard four weeks of it. My ears are tired.

LOMAX
(to herself)
God bless the plain-spoken.

Wren shifts his weight and says nothing. The gavel comes down once.

LOMAX (CONT'D)
We stand adjourned. Mr. Wren, open the doors.

He hauls on the silo's iron door. Light pours in, wheat-gold. The farmers on the bench rise and file out, caps in their hands.
